;;; A derived activator, where no run derives it: `19_derived_activator.ein`
;;; without `(item (a b))`.
;;;
;;; No fact puts a fact in `item`, and a candidate is a name, so no state
;;; derives an activator that binds `?R` to a fact. A rule *could* derive
;;; one, `item` being declared, and a declared relation's argument is any
;;; object, a fact among them (Q-M1f.41): the load says so in the warning
;;; its twin's load prints, and the program loads (Q-M1f.1). No run prints
;;; the second warning, since no state holds such an activator. The static
;;; check flags the program, as it flags its twin, so nothing is claimed of
;;; its answer (Q-M1f.564).
(relation box Node :why "{?3} is in the box")
(relation hit Node :why "{?3} is hit")
(relation item Node :why "{?3} is an item")
(box (a b))
(rule r (?R)
  :match (box (?R b))
  :assert (hit z)
  :why "a box holds a {?R} of b")
(rule mk ()
  :match (item ?f)
  :assert (r ?f)
  :why "{?f} is an item, so r is asked of it")
